    "narrative": "The offensive core for Ghost Ride the WHIP leans heavily on favorable matchups against mid-tier starters today, with **James Wood** (77.49) and **Tyler Soderstrom** (76.91) emerging as our premier daily assets. **James Wood** draws a high-upside matchup against Ritchie, buoyed by an elite xwOBA of 0.422 and a significant SP skill advantage. Meanwhile, **Tyler Soderstrom** benefits from a 10% park factor bump at home against Keider Montero. **Mickey Moniak** (75.75) rounds out our top-tier plays; despite facing a capable veteran in Seth Lugo, the 14% park boost and strong platoon advantage make him a mandatory start to chase those scarce stolen base opportunities. **Brice Turang** (70.83) remains a high-floor play, as his combination of leadoff-adjacent duties and an 85% success rate against the opposing SP's delivery makes him a key engine for our SB category.\n\nOur most difficult roster decision involved keeping **Seiya Suzuki** on the bench despite his decent 46.96 projection. Ultimately, the optimizer prioritized the specific situational upside of our starting outfielders over the risk associated with facing a tier-one arm like Gerrit Cole. **Seiya Suzuki** was hampered not only by the pitching matchup but also by negative wind conditions that suppressed his raw output. While **Austin Riley** (46.62) and **Cole Young** (45.66) hovered near the cutoff, they lacked the elite sprint speed or favorable park environments required to displace our current starters. We continue to monitor the status of **Vinnie Pasquantino**, who remains on the IL, leaving us to navigate the middle infield and corner spots with our healthy depth.\n\nManagers should remain alert to the \"Lineup Pending\" tags throughout the sheet. Players like **Dalton Rushing** (41.30) and **Jose Caballero** (47.71) are currently projected in less-than-ideal spots in the order; if either is pushed up the lineup, their efficiency scores will climb, though both currently face downward pressure from unfavorable wind or elite opposing catchers. **Corbin Carroll** (59.15) and **Gabriel Moreno** (46.11) both contend with G. Williams, an elite arm (2.99 xERA) that lowers their base projection significantly. While **Corbin Carroll** retains must-start status due to his stolen base potential, **Gabriel Moreno** sits at the lower end of our active tier and will need to capitalize on his #3 hole placement to justify his spot against a pitcher of that caliber.",
